45uhkDwLPyPYuRoPH_w9TZsYNmXkYPWODVhol1q/4vn/ftb1tYvyTF-4FIdERvPTUA/h26/h001=
.dGOEDdkXCbFf2ZrC8t-0cm0zsRWRvmw6ZIJ7ISYt8OQ" style=3D"color: #0077B6;">wro=
te this week of attending the Mo Ibrahim Foundation=E2=80=99s</a> governanc=
e weekend in Cairo. Like many conferences run and attended by Africans, the=
 view of the continent=E2=80=99s future is more optimistic, perhaps because=
 attenders are discussing it through an economist=E2=80=99s lens rather tha=
n a humanitarian one.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">He told me that when he asked one leading =
African businessman about the impact of aid cuts, he replied: =E2=80=9CF***=
 aid, we are past that.=E2=80=9D<br><em><strong style=3D"color: #052962; fo=
nt-style: normal; font-weight: 700;">Tracy McVeigh is editor of Foundations=
 and the Global development series</strong></em></p>
